const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How much water should a dog drink per day?',
    a: 'A common clinical teaching range for healthy adult dogs is about 50 to 60 milliliters of water per kilogram of body weight per day (roughly 1 to 1.3 ounces per pound). A 20 kg (44 lb) dog often lands near 1,000 to 1,200 ml daily under normal conditions. Heat, exercise, lactation, and dry food push needs higher. Use the range as a starting point and ask your veterinarian about your dog.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it bad if my dog drinks more than the chart?',
    a: 'Sometimes higher intake is normal—after a long hike, on a hot day, or when eating mostly dry kibble. Persistent, unexplained increases in thirst (polydipsia), especially with more urination, warrant a veterinary visit to check for diabetes, kidney disease, Cushing’s disease, or other causes. Do not restrict water to “force” a number on the chart.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How can I tell if my dog is dehydrated?',
    a: 'Mild clues include sticky gums, thick saliva, and lethargy. A skin tent on the shoulder that stays peaked, sunken eyes, or weak pulses are more serious. Dehydration can follow vomiting, diarrhea, heat, or not drinking. If you suspect dehydration, contact your veterinarian or an emergency clinic; do not rely on home fluid remedies as a substitute for exam.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I limit water at night for house training?',
    a: 'Brief, supervised routines for housetraining differ from restricting water for hours. Healthy dogs need free access to fresh water most of the day. Ask your veterinarian or trainer before withholding water, especially for puppies, seniors, or dogs with medical conditions.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do puppies need more water than adult dogs?',
    a: 'Puppies often drink frequently because they are growing, active, and may eat dry food. Per-kilogram needs can look similar to adults, but small puppies dehydrate faster if they vomit or have diarrhea. Offer fresh water often and call your veterinarian quickly if a puppy will not drink or has fluid losses. Adult charts are only a rough guide for them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does wet food reduce how much water my dog needs from the bowl?',
    a: 'Yes. Canned or fresh diets contain substantial moisture, so bowl drinking often falls while total water from food plus bowl stays adequate. Track overall hydration and urine habits, not bowl volume alone. Sudden refusal of both food and water is a reason to call your veterinarian.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can dogs drink too much water?',
    a: 'True water intoxication is uncommon but possible if a dog gulps huge volumes during play or retrieval in lakes. More often, “too much” drinking is a medical clue. If intake seems extreme or urine is constantly huge in volume, schedule a veterinary checkup rather than guessing.',
  },
];

const sources = [
  SRC.aspcaDogCare,
  SRC.msdDogHome,
  SRC.aahaNutrition,
